---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="About" current="about" description="What this atlas is, how it was made, what is measured and what is representative, and how to contribute.">
  <PageHead kicker="About" title="How this atlas was made" glyph="作">
    A hypothesis drawn carefully: what is measured, what is representative, and what is imagined.
  </PageHead>

  <article class="about">
    <section>
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Knots</em> is a long-running, open visualisation of a question: what, physically, is the thing a foam roller finds and a slow
        exhale releases? It is built around the essay
        <a href="https://ejhong.substack.com/p/knots-of-existence-hypotheses">Knots of Existence Hypotheses</a> (Eugene Jhong, 2026), its
        predecessor <a href="https://ejhong.substack.com/p/knots-of-existence-and-vasocomputation">Knots of Existence and Vasocomputation</a>,
        and the rival answers the literature and the traditions offer. It is not medical advice, and nothing here is a protocol.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is measured, and what is representative</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>The body</dt>
        <dd>
          The figure is the MakeHuman base mesh with its age targets (CC0), averaged across its ethnic targets and blended between female and
          male, from infancy to ninety. One level of Catmull–Clark subdivision smooths it at runtime.
        </dd>
        <dt>Perforators</dt>
        <dd>
          The counts are from the literature: an average of 374 major perforators (≥ 0.5 mm) per body (Taylor &amp; Palmer, 1987), organised
          in about forty source-artery territories, and on the order of a hundred thousand small perforators at 4–5 mm spacing (the essay’s
          estimate from skin area). Major perforators are placed more densely where surgeons find them clustered — the paraspinal and parasternal
          rows, the periumbilical field, the anterolateral thigh, near joints — and the rest by blue-noise sampling. <strong>Positions are
          representative, not a map of any one person</strong>: real perforators vary by centimetres between people and between sides.
        </dd>
        <dt>Trees</dt>
        <dd>
          Each perforator is joined to its nearest larger vessel and each major perforator to its root by shortest paths along the skin. Real
          source vessels run beneath the deep fascia; the trees here are drawn on the surface for legibility.
        </dd>
        <dt>Knots</dt>
        <dd>
          The simulation is a model of the hypothesis, not a measurement. Each site carries a vessel tone, a collar gel and a nerve state, coupled
          as the essay describes; timescales are compressed so that a release you can watch takes seconds. Where knots gather with age follows the
          essays’ predicted census — the junctions, the brace muscles, the roots at the ridges — which has never been measured.
        </dd>
        <dt>Maps</dt>
        <dd>
          Traditional maps are drawn from standard sources (for acupoints, the WHO Western Pacific standard) and placed by anatomical
          landmarks on this body. They are approximate and for comparison, not for needling.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>A caution</h2>
      <p>
        The base of the skull is anatomy, not a target: the suboccipital triangle sits over the vertebral artery, and deep pressure there is a
        known stroke risk. Forceful blowing, breath-holds and straining can drive air into the tissues of the face and chest. Intensive practice
        can destabilise for months or years. If something is happening to you, see a physician — electrolytes, creatine kinase, hydration,
        sleep — and slow down. For meditation-related difficulty, <a href="https://www.cheetahhouse.org">Cheetah House</a> is a place to start.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Open source</h2>
      <p>
        The code is MIT-licensed and the writing and data are CC BY 4.0, at
        <a href="https://github.com/ejhong/knots">github.com/ejhong/knots</a>. Hypotheses, maps and references are plain data files; each has a
        documented format, and new maps are placed by anatomical landmarks rather than coordinates. Corrections from anatomists, surgeons,
        clinicians and practitioners of the traditions are especially welcome.
      </p>
      <p class="mono lower faint">
        Built with Three.js and Astro. Type: Cormorant Garamond, EB Garamond, IBM Plex Mono, Shippori Mincho. Body: MakeHuman (CC0).
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: 68rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--gutter);
  }
  section {
    max-width: 44rem;
    padding: 3.5rem 0 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.4rem);
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }
  p,
  dd {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  em,
  strong {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  strong {
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  dl {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 8rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1.2rem 1.6rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
  dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.64rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    padding-top: 0.4rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    dl {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.3rem;
    }
    dd {
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
